{:edb/db-before-t 660 :edb/db-after-t 661 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533973 50 #inst "2026-10-05T22:44:41.757Z" 13194139533973 true] [17592186105646 1020 #edb/ref 17592186275459 13194139533973 true] [17592186105735 1020 #edb/ref 17592186275435 13194139533973 true] [17592186105735 1020 #edb/ref 17592186275442 13194139533973 true] [17592186105735 1020 #edb/ref 17592186275466 13194139533973 true] [17592186105736 1020 #edb/ref 17592186275446 13194139533973 true] [17592186275435 1017 #edb/ref 17592186275436 13194139533973 true] [17592186275435 1089 "q-115736" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275435 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533973 true] [17592186275435 1100 "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[-2,4].$ The right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $22.$ What must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $k$ | $5$ | $3$ |" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275435 1113 #uuid "98ae4888-9f4e-5350-a6dc-7fdb91edbc7f" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275435 1145 "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(2)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nLet's set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $k$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(k+5+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(k+8)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k+16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $A$ must be $22$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+16 & =22 \\\\ 2k & =22-16 \\\\ k & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275436 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275437 13194139533973 true] [17592186275436 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275438 13194139533973 true] [17592186275436 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275439 13194139533973 true] [17592186275436 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275440 13194139533973 true] [17592186275436 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275441 13194139533973 true] [17592186275436 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533973 true] [17592186275436 1041 "selection" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275436 1104 #edb/ref 17592186275437 13194139533973 true] [17592186275436 1110 #uuid "b44355b5-dcdc-57e1-86c4-4af0a4f69770" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275437 1034 "3" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275437 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533973 true] [17592186275437 1111 #uuid "f26ac8fd-24e5-5b0f-a3f2-f32c565b92d2" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275438 1034 "2" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275438 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533973 true] [17592186275438 1111 #uuid "a753eb55-eda9-5819-8868-27e498f2f52b" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275439 1034 "6" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275439 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533973 true] [17592186275439 1111 #uuid "e66f5fd5-c87c-5d87-b241-8da731327115" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275440 1034 "4" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275440 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533973 true] [17592186275440 1111 #uuid "89b3a5a4-aa1f-5236-96ba-aa2fbd44aa86" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275441 1034 "5" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275441 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533973 true] [17592186275441 1111 #uuid "30b76ce2-9d08-5ead-8903-e2bbd5860202" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275442 1017 #edb/ref 17592186275443 13194139533973 true] [17592186275442 1089 "q-346442" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275442 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533973 true] [17592186275442 1100 "| $x$ | $-1$ | $3$ | $7$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $4$ | $2$ | $k$ | $6$ |\n\nThe table above gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[-1,11]$ with a regular step size. If the right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $96$, then what must be the value of $k?$ \n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275442 1113 #uuid "a2a1eaff-a513-598a-8f28-b6c62e22c235" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275442 1145 "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(3)+f(7)+f(11)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $3$ | $7$ | $11$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $k$ | $6$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(2+k+6)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =(k+8)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =4k+32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A$ must be  $96,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4k+32 & =96 \\\\ 4k & =96-32 \\\\ k & =\\frac{64}{4} \\\\ k & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275443 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275444 13194139533973 true] [17592186275443 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275445 13194139533973 true] [17592186275443 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533973 true] [17592186275443 1041 "field-1" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275443 1104 #edb/ref 17592186275444 13194139533973 true] [17592186275443 1110 #uuid "85488c99-3ebe-5fd1-b862-8f9193f3487d" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275444 1034 "16" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275444 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533973 true] [17592186275444 1111 #uuid "a1e61861-8ff6-5e8d-b394-aee184004b21" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275445 1034 "18" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275445 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533973 true] [17592186275445 1111 #uuid "d9f77e0b-032d-577c-adde-d2eb73981d95" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275446 1017 #edb/ref 17592186275447 13194139533973 true] [17592186275446 1017 #edb/ref 17592186275452 13194139533973 true] [17592186275446 1017 #edb/ref 17592186275455 13194139533973 true] [17592186275446 1089 "q-346467" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275446 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533973 true] [17592186275446 1100 "Consider the area under the curve $y=(10-2x)^{2}$ over the interval $[2,4]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$The estimated area under the curve $y=(10-2x)^{2}$ over the interval $[2,4]$ is {{field-1}}.\nSince the function is strictly {{field-2}}\nin the interval,  the approximation is an\n{{field-3}}\nof the actual area." 13194139533973 true] [17592186275446 1113 #uuid "277725a3-237e-5051-ae07-4598fad2f8f4" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275446 1145 "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13950940/assets/1cb4c2c69c93117bd5eed39322059a3c0af84d16b293ccfc053c677ac0b6127d.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overset{\\text{heights}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(2.5)+f(3)+f(3.5)+f(4)}})\\cdot {{Δx}_{⏟}}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $2.5$ | $3$ | $3.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $25$ | $16$ | $9$ | $4$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(25+16+9+4)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =54\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =27\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{decreasing}$ over the interval $[2,4],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area." 13194139533973 true] [17592186275447 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275448 13194139533973 true] [17592186275447 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275449 13194139533973 true] [17592186275447 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275450 13194139533973 true] [17592186275447 1000 #edb/ref 17592186275451 13194139533973 true] [17592186275447 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139533973 true] [17592186275447 1041 "field-1" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275447 1104 #edb/ref 17592186275451 13194139533973 true] [17592186275447 1110 #uuid "40bcf070-a64e-5990-808a-a2db0461c83d" 13194139533973 true] [17592186275448 10